## Start

```sh
uv sync --group dev
uv run {{ cli_command }} --help
uv run {{ cli_command }} hello --name developer
uv run {{ cli_command }} show-config --path config.example.yml
```

Commit the generated `uv.lock` for repeatable dependency resolution and CI runs.

## Configuration and logging

```sh
uv run {{ cli_command }} config-path
uv run {{ cli_command }} init-config
uv run {{ cli_command }} show-config
uv run {{ cli_command }} set-config logging.level DEBUG
uv run {{ cli_command }} set-config logging.verbosity 2
uv run {{ cli_command }} hello --verbosity 1
```

Settings live in the platform-specific user configuration directory as `config.yml`.
On Linux this is typically `~/.config/{{ project_slug }}/config.yml`; use
`config-path` to resolve it on any platform. Missing user settings use in-memory
defaults. `init-config` never overwrites existing settings; `set-config` creates
the file if necessary and accepts dotted keys and YAML values (booleans, numbers,
strings, lists, and mappings). It preserves unrelated settings and comments.
Use `--path` on configuration commands (except `config-path`) or `--config`
on the sample `hello` command to select a different file.

```yaml
logging:
  level: INFO
  verbosity: 0
  file_enabled: true
```

`LoggingConfig` in `config.py` controls logging. Console verbosity is 0=WARNING,
1=INFO, 2=DEBUG, 3=TRACE. The file level is independent. File logs rotate at 5 MB
and old logs are retained for 7 days; disable file logging with `file_enabled: false`.
Logs go to stderr and the platform-specific log directory (`app.log`), not stdout.
The example `hello` command initializes logging at command execution; use the same
pattern in new business commands. Help and configuration commands do not create logs.
Do not store secrets in the YAML file or write them to logs.

## Environment diagnostics

```sh
uv run {{ cli_command }} doctor
uv run {{ cli_command }} doctor --json
```

Doctor checks the Python version, OS/architecture, YAML/logging configuration, writable
config, log and database paths, and SQLite runtime/schema. It never creates files or displays
configuration values. The SQLite check is an on-disk snapshot, not a live WAL or integrity
check. Any failed check gives exit status 1, with all results still emitted.

## Persistence

`database.py` uses Python's built-in sqlite3 with parameterized SQL, transactions,
foreign keys, a schema version and closed connections. The default database is
`data.sqlite3` in the platform-specific user data directory. Schema initialization
is lazy; help, diagnostics and config commands do not initialize it.
The starter key/value table demonstrates the persistence API; replace or extend it
with project-specific tables and explicit schema migrations.

```sh
uv run {{ cli_command }} db-path
uv run {{ cli_command }} db-set example value --json
uv run {{ cli_command }} db-get example --json
uv run {{ cli_command }} db-list --jsonl
```

## Output contract for agents

Pass these shared options after any application subcommand:

| Flag | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `--json` | One JSON document on stdout |
| `--jsonl` | One record per line; a single result is one line, an empty list emits nothing |
| `--quiet` | Suppress progress and diagnostic logging; retain results and errors |
| `--verbose` | Enable debug logging for business commands or verbose diagnostic notes |
| `--no-color` | Disable terminal ANSI styling |

JSON/JSONL automatically disable progress and color; they are mutually exclusive.
Quiet and verbose are mutually exclusive. Logs, progress and errors go to stderr;
commands exit nonzero on errors. Cyclopts help and parse errors remain textual.
The legacy numeric `hello --verbosity` option is retained. Verbose raises the
console level to at least DEBUG; quiet suppresses console logs, not file logs.
Machine output contains no Rich formatting; JSON is ASCII-escaped for portable
encoding and non-JSON values such as YAML dates are stringified.

For new commands, use the flattened `OutputOptions` parameter, initialize
`Output(options)`, enter `output.guard()` before side effects, and return results
through `output.emit(data)`. For long-running operations use `output.track()`
or `output.progress()`; both render on stderr only for interactive human output.

## Develop

```sh
uv run pytest -q
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format --check .
uv build
```

`src/{{ package_name }}/cli.py` is the command interface. Put logic in separate modules and test those first.
See `AGENTS.md` for TDD and environment isolation conventions.

## Automation

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` delegates PR validation and distribution-building to
[RuchirChawdhry/ci-workflows](https://github.com/RuchirChawdhry/ci-workflows).
It runs for pull requests, not pushes or schedules. No PyPI publishing is enabled.

